Pre-scales register operands to byte offsets (X2)

The register backend stored every register operand as a register index and
scaled it by the 16-byte register size at each use, so the handlers carried a
shift on the hot path -- `shl $0x4` appeared 1380 times in the binary and three
times in regvm_t_add alone. Scaling once, at encode time, and storing the byte
offset removes the shift from every handler.

  * quickjs.c: the register access macros (FR/FRP) add the frame base instead of
    indexing and scaling, and 78 direct fr[...] uses were rewritten to them; the
    encoder, the verifier and the narrow-operand selection scale once; the
    monolith's RD_REG/RD_REG8 decode macros drop their shift; the bytecode
    reader bounds reg_count by the widest representable register offset rather
    than by UINT16_MAX, and validates a register operand as an aligned byte
    offset rather than as an index.
  * quickjs.c: two narrow handlers added by 0115 (L_ROP_load_i8_s and
    L_ROP_move_s) read their register operands with RD_U8 rather than RD_REG8,
    which the old unshifted encoding made harmless and this one makes wrong;
    they now use RD_REG8 like every other narrow handler.
  * quickjs-regvm.h: REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION 12 -> 13.

Bytecode version bump
  Not here. This changes the register format only and leaves BC_VERSION alone;
  the precompiled register blobs record REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION in their header, so
  they are regenerated in the staged 9999 candidate, as they were for the 0115
  and 0116 register format changes.
